Good point.  I never go off to see the wizard without my dragon scale
mail, so I did not he was capable of a touch of death or of destroying
my armor.  Realistically, I see how the armor (or intrinsic magic
resistance) would protect my person from magic attacks but how does it
protect my boots or my cloak?  Give the wiz a fighting chance; he
should be able to destroy everything except my dragon scale armor and
my Hawaiian shirt.  If I wear a cloak of magic resistance, then it, my
armor, my Hawaiian shirt, and my person should be protected.

Suggestion for a new character class: the DeadHead.  Starts with a
tie-died shirt, shorts, a syringe, and a amulet (random or life
protection).  The tie-died shirt should cause attacking monsters to
hallucinate; hallucinating monsters would not know whether to attack
you.  Syringe attacks would cause death by overdose (3 turn delay),
death by viral infection (100 turns), peacefulness, tameness, or
hallucination.  Hallucinating creatures would, of course, be arrested
by the keystone cops.  Any creature carrying a syringe would be
executed.  Thus you might gain an advantage by selling your syringe to
a shopkeeper and then robbing him.  The kops would execute him instead
of you!

You think you hear someone cursing shopkeepers.

